 {"id":"7.1","q":"For doing the SWOT analysis of a company, which of the following could be the first approach?","o":["Identifying strengths and weaknesses","Identifying strengths and opportunities","Identifying weaknesses and threats","Identifying opportunities and weaknesses"],"a":0,"w":"Book sample question. The book's first approach identifies strengths and weaknesses first, then the related opportunities and threats."},
 {"id":"7.2","q":"According to the book, the most prominent question in the research checklist is:","o":["Do I understand the business?","What is the target price?","How many shares does the promoter hold?","What is the dividend yield?"],"a":0,"w":"No analyst should move on if they cannot say what the company does in one clear line."},
 {"id":"7.3","q":"\"Wide diversification is only required when investors do not understand what they are doing\" is a quote from:","o":["Warren Buffett","Charlie Munger","Peter Lynch","Gary Hamel"],"a":0,"w":"The book quotes Buffett in section 7.2."},
 {"id":"7.4","q":"As per the book, average room tariff is an important evaluation parameter for which sector?","o":["Hotels","Retail","Banking","Telecom"],"a":0,"w":"Retail: footfalls and SSS. Banking: NII and NIM. Telecom: ARPU."},
 {"id":"7.5","q":"Which of the following is a company-specific factor that drives pricing power?","o":["Brand affinity among customers","Competition intensity in the industry","Price elasticity of the product","Level of commoditisation of the product"],"a":0,"w":"The other three are industry factors that affect all players."},
 {"id":"7.6","q":"Smaller petrochemical makers price their products based on prices set by Reliance Industries. This gives RIL pricing power because of its:","o":["Natural leadership position","High fixed cost","Customer concentration","Government ownership"],"a":0,"w":"The book's example of a natural industry leader."},
 {"id":"7.7","q":"Competing by keeping prices low is sustainable only if the company:","o":["Has a low-cost advantage","Has the most advertising","Has the largest number of products","Has a high dividend payout"],"a":0,"w":"Without lower costs, competitors can copy the price cut."},
 {"id":"7.8","q":"To execute a product differentiation strategy, a company should have:","o":["A strong R&amp;D team and a culture of innovation","The lowest price in the market","A high level of debt","A large number of independent directors"],"a":0,"w":"Product differentiation depends on innovation."},
 {"id":"7.9","q":"According to the book, execution capability can be studied by looking at:","o":["The past track record of the company and its management","Only the latest quarterly results","The company's logo and website","The number of shareholders"],"a":0,"w":"Track record is the book's suggested evidence."},
 {"id":"7.10","q":"In SWOT analysis, strengths and weaknesses are ______, while opportunities and threats are ______.","o":["Internal; external","External; internal","Financial; non-financial","Short term; long term"],"a":0,"w":"Strengths and weaknesses are internal to the company; opportunities and threats come from outside."},
 {"id":"7.11","q":"For an external observer like an equity analyst, the more suitable SWOT approach is to:","o":["Identify opportunities and threats first","Identify strengths and weaknesses first","Identify only threats","Skip SWOT and use only ratios"],"a":0,"w":"It follows the E-I-C order: outside conditions first, then the company."},
 {"id":"7.12","q":"High customer concentration in a company should be classified in SWOT as a:","o":["Weakness","Threat","Opportunity","Strength"],"a":0,"w":"It is a risk, but it is internal, so it is a weakness rather than a threat."},
 {"id":"7.13","q":"Which of the following is listed in the book as a strength?","o":["Support from parent company or government","High fixed cost","Significant legal cases","Low margins"],"a":0,"w":"The other three are listed as weaknesses."},
 {"id":"7.14","q":"When Jet Airways suspended operations in April 2019, it created an opportunity for other airlines. This is an example of:","o":["Consolidation opportunity arising from adverse market conditions","A threat from deregulation","A weakness of the other airlines","Technological disruption"],"a":0,"w":"The book cites it as adverse conditions creating opportunities for consolidation."},
 {"id":"7.15","q":"RBI's decision to offer on-tap licences to new banks is cited in the book as an example of:","o":["A threat to existing banks from deregulation","An opportunity for existing banks","A strength of existing banks","A weakness of new banks"],"a":0,"w":"Deregulation can remove entry barriers and raise competition."},
 {"id":"7.16","q":"According to the book, artificial intelligence is a threat to which industry?","o":["BPO industry doing repetitive tasks","Cement industry","Hotel industry","Steel industry"],"a":0,"w":"Technological disruption favouring one industry can threaten another."},
 {"id":"7.17","q":"The separation of ownership and management in a company creates:","o":["Agency risk","Currency risk","Liquidity risk","Reinvestment risk"],"a":0,"w":"Shareholders rely on managers who may pursue their own interests or lack capability."},
 {"id":"7.18","q":"According to the book, why should analysts focus on the corporate governance structure?","o":["Because analysing integrity directly is almost impossible","Because it is the only public information","Because competency does not matter","Because regulators require analysts to do so"],"a":0,"w":"Analysts check whether controls exist to prevent inappropriate governance actions."},
 {"id":"7.19","q":"Highly concentrated decision making in a company creates:","o":["Key-man risk","Credit risk","Basis risk","Inflation risk"],"a":0,"w":"Churn in top management can derail decisions. Delegation and succession plans reduce it."},
 {"id":"7.20","q":"A management team that fails to meet regulatory compliance on time indicates, according to the book:","o":["It may not be in control, and it is a red flag on integrity","Strong delegation","Good execution","Nothing of concern"],"a":0,"w":"The book treats compliance failure as a sign of poor control and a red flag."},
 {"id":"7.21","q":"As per the book, if the chairman is an executive director, independent directors should constitute at least ______ of the board.","o":["50%","One third","Two thirds","25%"],"a":0,"w":"In other cases, at least one third."},
 {"id":"7.22","q":"As per the book, SEBI regulation requires at least ______ of audit committee members to be independent.","o":["Two thirds","One third","50%","All"],"a":0,"w":"Ideally all should be independent; the regulation requires two thirds."},
 {"id":"7.23","q":"According to the book, an auditor's remuneration from a group or entity for all services should be less than ______ of the auditor's overall income.","o":["10%","25%","5%","50%"],"a":0,"w":"To avoid over-dependence on one client's fees."},
 {"id":"7.24","q":"According to the book, auditors should be rotated once in:","o":["Five years","Two years","Ten years","Every year"],"a":0,"w":"Rotation gives a chance to uncover facts concealed with an auditor's help."},
 {"id":"7.25","q":"As per the book, SEBI regulation on the remuneration committee stipulates that:","o":["All members should be non-executive directors and the chairman should be independent","All members should be executive directors","The CEO should chair the committee","At least one member should be the auditor"],"a":0,"w":"Ideally the committee would be entirely independent."},
 {"id":"7.26","q":"As per the book, what does SEBI regulation require for related party transactions?","o":["All related party transactions should be placed before the audit committee","All need pre-approval of shareholders in a general meeting","They are banned","They need approval of the stock exchange"],"a":0,"w":"Pre-approval is ideal but not mandated; justification is needed if not at arm's length."},
 {"id":"7.27","q":"Which statement about pledging of shares by promoters is correct as per the book?","o":["Pledging does not necessarily signal a problem, but a high level of pledged shares can aggravate market risk","Pledging always indicates fraud","Pledged shares cannot be sold by lenders","Pledging reduces the promoter's legal ownership to zero"],"a":0,"w":"A price fall can force lenders to liquidate, adding downward pressure."},
 {"id":"7.28","q":"When promoters pledge shares, lenders lend less than the market value of the shares. This difference is called a:","o":["Haircut","Premium","Coupon","Basis"],"a":0,"w":"The haircut protects lenders if the price falls."},
 {"id":"7.29","q":"Which of the following is a negative of a strong promoter shareholding for minority shareholders?","o":["Promoters may influence actions such as related party transactions that benefit them","Management is more likely to act in shareholders' interest","Promoters have higher control on management","Promoters are founders of the company"],"a":0,"w":"The book lists this as the risk side of promoter influence."},
 {"id":"7.30","q":"Credit ratings are relevant to equity investors because:","o":["Equity investors get returns only after lenders are serviced","Ratings set the share price","Ratings are given only to equity shares","Ratings measure dividend yield"],"a":0,"w":"Ratings show financial risk and so shape return expectations."},
 {"id":"7.31","q":"Reading the history of a company's credit ratings can tell an analyst:","o":["How responsive management is to external feedback","The exact future share price","The auditor's fees","The promoter's personal wealth"],"a":0,"w":"If concerns in past rating reports were addressed, management responds well to feedback."},
 {"id":"7.32","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the financial advantages of ESG cited in the book?","o":["Guaranteed higher share price","Minimum disruption from regulatory intervention or environmental activism","Easier recruitment and customer attraction","Lower cost of capital from strong governance"],"a":0,"w":"No guarantee of share price is claimed. ESG is a filter, followed by regular analysis."},
 {"id":"7.33","q":"According to the book, SEBI named the top ______ listed companies to make ESG disclosures as per BRSR from FY2023.","o":["1,000","500","100","4,000"],"a":0,"w":"In FY22, more than 175 companies reported on BRSR voluntarily."},
 {"id":"7.34","q":"According to the book, the most easily available, reliable and consistent source of information on a company is:","o":["Annual and quarterly reports","Media reports","Management interviews on the internet","Competitors' websites"],"a":0,"w":"The book describes annual and quarterly reports this way."},
 {"id":"7.35","q":"Promoters who state that nothing could go wrong in their business fall into the category of:","o":["People who don't know that they don't know","Risk-averse entrepreneurs","Independent directors","Value investors"],"a":0,"w":"The book says such promoters should be avoided."},
